window.COURSE={"pages":[{"key":"home","file":"patterns/index.html","name":"Design Patterns home","short":"DP","band":"","sections":[]},{"key":"p00","file":"patterns/00-foundations.html","name":"P00 · Foundations and SOLID","short":"P00","band":"ptf","sections":[{"id":"pf-what","n":"01","t":"What is a design pattern (and what is it not)?"},{"id":"pf-families","n":"02","t":"The three families, and the five moves of this course"},{"id":"pf-iface","n":"03","t":"Interface vs abstract class"},{"id":"pf-uml","n":"04","t":"Reading a class diagram"},{"id":"pf-prog","n":"05","t":"Program to an interface, not an implementation"},{"id":"pf-dispatch","n":"06","t":"How Java picks the method: overriding and dynamic dispatch"},{"id":"pf-comp","n":"07","t":"Composition over inheritance"},{"id":"pf-srp","n":"08","t":"SOLID · S: Single Responsibility"},{"id":"pf-ocp","n":"09","t":"SOLID · O: Open / Closed"},{"id":"pf-lsp","n":"10","t":"SOLID · L: Liskov Substitution"},{"id":"pf-isp","n":"11","t":"SOLID · I: Interface Segregation"},{"id":"pf-dip","n":"12","t":"SOLID · D: Dependency Inversion"},{"id":"pf-lambda","n":"13","t":"Lambdas are tiny objects: functional interfaces, anonymous classes, method references"},{"id":"pf-refactor","n":"14","t":"Your first refactor: when a pattern starts to pay off"},{"id":"pf-qa","n":"15","t":"13 interview questions"},{"id":"pf-review","n":"16","t":"Review"}]},{"key":"p01","file":"patterns/01-strategy.html","name":"P01 · Strategy","short":"P01","band":"p01","sections":[{"id":"stg-see","n":"01","t":"See it: the problem Strategy solves"},{"id":"stg-build","n":"02","t":"Build it: from the if / else ladder to Strategy"},{"id":"stg-run","n":"03","t":"Watch it run: who calls whom"},{"id":"stg-practise","n":"04","t":"Practise: add a rule, refactor a ladder"},{"id":"stg-choose","n":"05","t":"Choose it: roles, look-alikes and trade-offs"},{"id":"stg-master","n":"06","t":"Master it: Strategy in modern Java"},{"id":"stg-review","n":"07","t":"Interview and review"}]},{"key":"p02","file":"patterns/02-template-method.html","name":"P02 · Template Method","short":"P02","band":"p02","sections":[{"id":"tpl-see","n":"01","t":"See it: one inspection, three copies"},{"id":"tpl-build","n":"02","t":"Build it: from copies to one template"},{"id":"tpl-run","n":"03","t":"Watch it run: the parent calls the child"},{"id":"tpl-practise","n":"04","t":"Practise"},{"id":"tpl-choose","n":"05","t":"Choose it: when, when not, and its look-alikes"},{"id":"tpl-master","n":"06","t":"Master it: lambdas, default methods and the traps"},{"id":"tpl-review","n":"07","t":"Interview and review"}]},{"key":"p03","file":"patterns/03-state.html","name":"P03 · State","short":"P03","band":"p03","sections":[{"id":"sta-see","n":"01","t":"See it: an order that behaves differently at every stage"},{"id":"sta-build","n":"02","t":"Build it: from status switches to state classes"},{"id":"sta-run","n":"03","t":"Watch it run: every call, in order"},{"id":"sta-practise","n":"04","t":"Practise: change the lifecycle yourself"},{"id":"sta-choose","n":"05","t":"Choose it: when State beats a switch, and when it does not"},{"id":"sta-master","n":"06","t":"Master it: enums, sealed types and shared states"},{"id":"sta-review","n":"07","t":"Interview and review"}]},{"key":"p04","file":"patterns/04-factory-method.html","name":"P04 · Factory Method","short":"P04","band":"p04","sections":[{"id":"fac-see","n":"01","t":"See it: the problem Factory Method solves"},{"id":"fac-build","n":"02","t":"Build it: from scattered new to Factory Method"},{"id":"fac-run","n":"03","t":"Watch it run: who creates what"},{"id":"fac-practise","n":"04","t":"Practise: add a showroom, test with a fake"},{"id":"fac-choose","n":"05","t":"Choose it: roles, look-alikes and trade-offs"},{"id":"fac-master","n":"06","t":"Master it: Factory Method in modern Java"},{"id":"fac-review","n":"07","t":"Interview and review"}]},{"key":"p05","file":"patterns/05-builder.html","name":"P05 · Builder","short":"P05","band":"p05","sections":[{"id":"bld-see","n":"01","t":"See it: the problem Builder solves"},{"id":"bld-build","n":"02","t":"Build it: from telescoping constructors to a Builder"},{"id":"bld-run","n":"03","t":"Watch it run: the order form becomes a car"},{"id":"bld-practise","n":"04","t":"Practise: add an option, add a rule"},{"id":"bld-choose","n":"05","t":"Choose it: roles, look-alikes and trade-offs"},{"id":"bld-master","n":"06","t":"Master it: Builder in modern Java"},{"id":"bld-review","n":"07","t":"Interview and review"}]},{"key":"p06","file":"patterns/06-singleton.html","name":"P06 · Singleton","short":"P06","band":"p06","sections":[{"id":"sgl-see","n":"01","t":"See it: the problem Singleton solves"},{"id":"sgl-build","n":"02","t":"Build it: from new everywhere to one instance"},{"id":"sgl-run","n":"03","t":"Watch it run: three desks, one object"},{"id":"sgl-practise","n":"04","t":"Practise: make one, then make it testable"},{"id":"sgl-choose","n":"05","t":"Choose it: when one shared object is right"},{"id":"sgl-master","n":"06","t":"Master it: threads, reflection and enums"},{"id":"sgl-review","n":"07","t":"Interview and review"}]}],"qa":[{"k":"pf-qa:01","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"What is a design pattern? Why use one?","a":"A named, reusable solution to a design problem that keeps recurring, described as roles for classes and objects rather than finished code. It gives a team a shared vocabulary (\"use a Strategy\"), reuses designs that are known to work, and makes code easier to change in the direction the pattern plans for. The cost is extra classes and indirection, so use one only when the problem is really there."},{"k":"pf-qa:02","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"What are the three categories of GoF patterns? Give two of each.","a":"<b>Creational</b> (how objects are created): Singleton, Factory Method, Builder, Abstract Factory, Prototype. <b>Structural</b> (how objects are composed): Adapter, Decorator, Proxy, Facade, Composite, Bridge, Flyweight. <b>Behavioral</b> (how objects interact and share responsibility): Strategy, Observer, Command, State, Template Method, Chain of Responsibility, Iterator, Mediator, Memento, Visitor, Interpreter."},{"k":"pf-qa:03","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"Interface vs abstract class: when do you choose which?","a":"An interface defines a capability that unrelated classes can share; a class can implement many; it has no instance state (only constants) and no constructor, though since Java 8 it can have default and static methods. An abstract class is a partial base for closely related classes that share state and code; a class can extend only one. Choose an interface for the type you program against, and add an abstract class underneath when several implementations share real code."},{"k":"pf-qa:04","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"What does \"program to an interface, not an implementation\" mean?","a":"Declare fields, parameters and return types with the interface (<code>List</code>, <code>PaymentGateway</code>) and name the concrete class only where the object is created. The using code then works with any implementation, including future ones and test fakes, and the choice can change in one place."},{"k":"pf-qa:05","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"Composition vs inheritance: why \"favour composition\"?","a":"Inheritance is fixed at compile time, exposes the parent's internals to the child (fragile base class), and multiplies classes when features combine (bodies × engines). Composition keeps each feature in its own class behind an interface, lets the owner delegate to it, and lets you swap parts at run time. Use inheritance only for a true, substitutable is-a relationship."},{"k":"pf-qa:06","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"What is SOLID? One line for each letter.","a":"<b>S</b>ingle responsibility: one reason to change. <b>O</b>pen/closed: extend by adding code, not by editing it. <b>L</b>iskov substitution: subtypes must be usable wherever the parent is. <b>I</b>nterface segregation: no client depends on methods it does not use. <b>D</b>ependency inversion: high and low level both depend on abstractions."},{"k":"pf-qa:07","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"Does Single Responsibility mean a class should have one method?","a":"No. It means one <b>reason to change</b>: the class answers to one actor or one concern. An <code>Invoice</code> record with several methods about the invoice's own rules is fine; printing and storage are different concerns and belong elsewhere."},{"k":"pf-qa:08","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"Give an example of a Liskov violation, and how you would spot one.","a":"<code>ElectricCar extends Car</code> where <code>refuel()</code> throws <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code>, or the classic <code>Square extends Rectangle</code> that breaks <code>setWidth</code>. Signs: overrides that throw or do nothing, callers that check <code>instanceof</code> before calling, and subclasses that need stricter inputs than the parent. Fix it by changing the abstraction so every subtype can keep its promise."},{"k":"pf-qa:09","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"Dependency Inversion vs Dependency Injection vs IoC?","a":"DIP is the principle (depend on abstractions, not concretions). DI is a technique that applies it (pass the dependency in through the constructor, a setter or a field). IoC is the general idea that a framework controls object creation and the flow of calls; Spring's IoC container creates beans and performs the injection."},{"k":"pf-qa:10","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"Can you overuse design patterns?","a":"Yes. A pattern adds interfaces, classes and indirection; applied to a problem you do not have, it makes simple code harder to read (often called \"patternitis\"). Start simple, and introduce a pattern when the second or third variation actually appears, or when a SOLID principle is clearly being broken. Interviewers like to hear you say when <i>not</i> to use one."},{"k":"pf-qa:11","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"Overloading vs overriding: which is decided when?","a":"An <b>override</b> has the same name and parameter types as a parent or interface method; the JVM picks it at <b>run time</b> from the object's class (dynamic dispatch). An <b>overload</b> has the same name but different parameter types; the compiler picks it at <b>compile time</b> from the declared types of the arguments. So with <code>Car car = new ElectricCar()</code>, <code>car.describe()</code> runs ElectricCar's override, but <code>welcome(car)</code> calls the <code>welcome(Car)</code> overload. Static, private and final methods are never overridden."},{"k":"pf-qa:12","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"What is a functional interface?","a":"An interface with exactly <b>one abstract method</b> (default and static methods don't count, nor do public <code>Object</code> methods such as <code>equals</code>). A lambda or method reference can implement it, because the compiler knows which method the code is for. <code>@FunctionalInterface</code> is optional but makes the compiler reject a second abstract method. JDK examples: <code>Runnable</code>, <code>Comparator</code>, <code>Function</code>, <code>Supplier</code>, <code>Consumer</code>, <code>Predicate</code>."},{"k":"pf-qa:13","l":"P00","sec":"pf-qa","st":"13 interview questions","q":"Anonymous class vs lambda: what is the difference?","a":"Both create an object that implements an interface in place. In an anonymous class <code>this</code> is the anonymous object itself; in a lambda <code>this</code> is the enclosing object. An anonymous class can hold fields, have several methods, extend a class or implement a multi-method interface; a lambda only implements a functional interface and has no state of its own. Each anonymous class is a separate <code>.class</code> file; lambdas are linked at run time through <code>invokedynamic</code>. Both may capture only effectively final local variables."},{"k":"stg-review:01","l":"P01","sec":"stg-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"What is the Strategy pattern? (the 30-second answer)","a":"<b>B ·</b> Strategy defines a family of interchangeable algorithms, puts each one in its own class behind a common interface, and lets the client choose which one a context object uses. The context holds a reference to the interface and delegates to it, so new behaviour is added with a new class instead of a new if / else. Example: <code>Checkout</code> delegating to a <code>Discount</code> such as <code>FestivalDiscount</code>; in the JDK, <code>List.sort(Comparator)</code>."},{"k":"stg-review:02","l":"P01","sec":"stg-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Walk me through it in more depth.","a":"<b>I ·</b> Three roles: the <b>Strategy</b> interface (one operation, e.g. <code>apply(price)</code>), <b>ConcreteStrategies</b> implementing it, and the <b>Context</b> that has-a Strategy and calls it for the varying step. The <b>client</b> picks the concrete strategy (constructor, setter, factory, map or DI). Benefits: Open / Closed (new rule = new class), Single Responsibility (one rule per class), easy testing, run-time swapping. Costs: more classes, the client must know the options, and the strategy interface must fit every variant (if one rule needs extra data, pass a richer parameter object). In modern Java a single-method strategy is a functional interface, so lambdas and method references are strategies too."},{"k":"stg-review:03","l":"P01","sec":"stg-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Strategy vs State: the diagrams look the same. What is the difference?","a":"<b>I ·</b> Intent and who chooses. In Strategy the <b>client</b> picks the behaviour from outside and the strategies don't know each other (any discount for any checkout). In State the <b>object itself</b> changes its state object as events happen (an order goes booked → paid → delivered), and states usually know which state comes next. Strategy answers \"how should this be done?\"; State answers \"what can I do right now, given where I am?\"."},{"k":"stg-review:04","l":"P01","sec":"stg-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How do you choose the strategy at run time?","a":"<b>I ·</b> Something at the edge maps an input to a strategy, so the context never branches. Options: a <b>factory</b> method with one switch; a <b>Map&lt;String, Strategy&gt;</b> registry (getOrDefault or a clear error for unknown keys); an <b>enum</b> whose constants hold the strategies; or <b>dependency injection</b>: Spring injects all <code>DiscountPolicy</code> beans as a <code>List</code> or a <code>Map</code> keyed by bean name, and the service picks by key. The choice can come from the request, configuration, a feature flag or the database."},{"k":"stg-review:05","l":"P01","sec":"stg-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"With lambdas, is Strategy still a pattern?","a":"<b>A ·</b> Yes. The pattern is the design (a context delegating to an interchangeable behaviour supplied from outside), not the class count. Lambdas make concrete strategies cheap when the interface has one method and the rule has no state or name. You still want classes or records when the strategy has data, several methods, dependencies, or deserves its own tests and a name in logs. Many people say \"in functional style Strategy is just passing a function\": that is the same idea, which is why <code>Comparator</code> works both ways."},{"k":"stg-review:06","l":"P01","sec":"stg-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How does Strategy follow Open / Closed and Dependency Inversion?","a":"<b>B ·</b> Open / Closed: the context is closed for modification (no branch per rule) and open for extension (add a class that implements the interface). Dependency Inversion: the high-level <code>Checkout</code> depends on the abstraction <code>Discount</code>, not on <code>FestivalDiscount</code>; the low-level rules depend on the same abstraction, and the concrete one is injected from outside. It also helps Single Responsibility: each rule has one reason to change."},{"k":"stg-review:07","l":"P01","sec":"stg-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Strategy vs Template Method?","a":"<b>I ·</b> Both vary part of an algorithm. Template Method uses <b>inheritance</b>: a base class fixes the steps and subclasses override some; the choice is fixed when you pick the subclass at compile time. Strategy uses <b>composition</b>: the whole varying algorithm is a separate object, swappable at run time and combinable. Prefer Strategy when variants must be mixed or switched; Template Method when the skeleton is fixed and only a few steps differ."},{"k":"stg-review:08","l":"P01","sec":"stg-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"When would you NOT use Strategy?","a":"<b>B ·</b> When there are two or three stable cases (a plain if / else or switch is clearer), when variants differ only by a value (use a parameter), or when nobody needs to swap or test the variants separately. Adding interfaces for change that never comes is over-engineering; refactor to Strategy when the third variant arrives or when you need a fake in a test."},{"k":"stg-review:09","l":"P01","sec":"stg-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Can strategies be shared between threads or be singletons?","a":"<b>A ·</b> Stateless strategies can: one instance for the whole application (Spring singleton beans, enum constants, static lambdas) is safe and avoids allocation. A strategy with mutable fields is not safe to share; make it immutable, create one per use, or synchronise. Watch the context too: a <code>setStrategy</code> on an object shared by threads is a race, so prefer a final field and a new context per request."},{"k":"tpl-review:01","l":"P02","sec":"tpl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"What is the Template Method pattern? (30-second answer)","a":"<b>B ·</b> A behavioral pattern where a base class defines the skeleton of an algorithm in one method, the template method, usually <code>final</code>, and subclasses override only the steps that vary. Fixed steps stay in the base, required steps are abstract, optional ones are hooks with a default. Example: <code>InputStream.read(byte[], int, int)</code> built on the abstract <code>read()</code>."},{"k":"tpl-review:02","l":"P02","sec":"tpl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Explain it in more depth, with an example from your work.","a":"<b>I ·</b> Our pre-delivery inspection had a class per fuel type with the same five steps copied, and the copies drifted. I moved the order into an abstract <code>PreDeliveryInspection.inspect()</code>, marked it <code>final</code>, made the fixed steps private, made the model checks abstract and the road test a hook. New fuel type = one subclass with one method. It applies the Hollywood principle (the base calls the subclass) and Open/Closed for the steps. The cost is inheritance coupling, so if the steps had needed to change at run time I would have used Strategy, or lambdas, instead."},{"k":"tpl-review:03","l":"P02","sec":"tpl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Why is the template method usually final?","a":"<b>B ·</b> Because the order <b>is</b> the pattern. If a subclass could override <code>inspect()</code>, it could skip or reorder steps (sign off before the road test) and the base class could no longer guarantee anything. <code>final</code> lets the compiler enforce the skeleton; subclasses vary only the steps they are allowed to."},{"k":"tpl-review:04","l":"P02","sec":"tpl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Hook vs abstract step: what is the difference?","a":"<b>B ·</b> An abstract step (primitive operation) has no body, so every concrete subclass <b>must</b> implement it. A hook has a default body (empty, or <code>return true</code>), so a subclass <b>may</b> override it to add or skip behaviour. Use abstract for what truly differs per subclass, hooks for optional extension points."},{"k":"tpl-review:05","l":"P02","sec":"tpl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Template Method vs Strategy?","a":"<b>I ·</b> Both vary part of a behaviour. Template Method uses inheritance: the base class controls the flow and subclasses override steps; the variant is fixed when you choose which class to create. Strategy uses composition: the context holds an interface field and calls it; the variant can be swapped at run time and combined freely. Template Method varies <i>steps</i> of one algorithm, Strategy usually the whole algorithm. Modern Java blurs it: passing the steps as lambdas turns a template into a strategy."},{"k":"tpl-review:06","l":"P02","sec":"tpl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"What is the fragile base class problem here?","a":"<b>I ·</b> Subclasses depend on the base class's internals: which steps it calls, in which order, with what state. A harmless-looking change to the base (call <code>roadTest()</code> twice, rename a hook, change a default) silently changes every subclass. Mitigate it: keep the template <code>final</code>, keep fixed steps private, keep the number of protected steps small, document what each hook may assume, and test the base with a minimal subclass."},{"k":"tpl-review:07","l":"P02","sec":"tpl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How many subclasses before you would switch to Strategy?","a":"<b>I ·</b> It is not a number but a shape. A handful of stable subclasses that each override one or two steps is fine. Switch when the choice must happen at run time, when variations combine (each new dimension multiplies subclasses), when subclasses start overriding hooks just to switch things off, or when you want to test a step without the base class. Many teams start to move around the third independent variation."},{"k":"tpl-review:08","l":"P02","sec":"tpl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Where do you see Template Method in Java and Spring?","a":"<b>A ·</b> <code>InputStream.read(byte[],int,int)</code> on <code>read()</code>; <code>AbstractList</code> (implement <code>get</code> and <code>size</code>); <code>HttpServlet.service()</code> dispatching to <code>doGet</code>/<code>doPost</code> (not <code>final</code>, so it can be overridden); Spring's <code>OncePerRequestFilter</code> (<code>final doFilter</code> → <code>doFilterInternal</code>) and <code>AbstractApplicationContext.refresh()</code> with hooks like <code>onRefresh()</code>. <code>JdbcTemplate</code> is the callback variant: the fixed JDBC steps are in the template, the varying step (a <code>RowMapper</code>) is passed in, not overridden."},{"k":"tpl-review:09","l":"P02","sec":"tpl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Why should the base class constructor not call a step?","a":"<b>A ·</b> Java runs the base constructor before the subclass's field initialisers. If it calls an overridden step, the subclass method runs on a half-built object and sees its fields as <code>null</code> or 0. Run the template from a normal method after construction."},{"k":"sta-review:01","l":"P03","sec":"sta-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Explain the State pattern in 30 seconds.","a":"State lets an object change its behaviour when its internal stage changes. The object, called the context, holds a reference to a state object and forwards every request to it. Each stage is its own class implementing a common interface; it handles the request and, when needed, switches the context to the next state. Example: a car order moving Booked → Paid → Delivered, where cancel refunds from Paid and is rejected after delivery, with no switch statements."},{"k":"sta-review:02","l":"P03","sec":"sta-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Give the deeper answer: structure, benefits, costs.","a":"Roles: <b>Context</b> (holds <code>state</code>, delegates, exposes a narrow way to change state), <b>State</b> interface (one method per request; defaults or an abstract base hold the \"not allowed\" answer), <b>ConcreteStates</b> (one per stage, implementing allowed requests and choosing successors). Benefits: no conditionals on a status code, each stage's rules in one class, new stages added as classes (Open / Closed), illegal actions handled in one place, each state testable alone. Costs: more classes, states coupled to their successors and to the context's API, transitions spread over classes (draw the state diagram). Alternatives: an enum with per-constant methods when stages are fixed, a sealed hierarchy with pattern-matching switches when states carry data, a state-machine library for configurable workflows."},{"k":"sta-review:03","l":"P03","sec":"sta-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"State vs Strategy: the class diagrams are identical. What is the difference?","a":"<b>B ·</b> Intent and who changes the object. With Strategy the <b>client</b> chooses one algorithm from outside, and the strategies don't know each other (a discount rule). With State the <b>states themselves</b> replace each other as the object moves through its lifecycle, usually many times, and each state knows its successors. Strategy answers \"how should this be done?\", State answers \"what can I do now, and what happens next?\"."},{"k":"sta-review:04","l":"P03","sec":"sta-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Who should own the transitions: the context or the states?","a":"<b>I ·</b> Both are valid GoF variants. <b>States own them</b> (as here): adding a stage touches only its neighbours, but states depend on each other. <b>The context owns them</b> (a transition table in <code>Order</code>): the whole lifecycle is visible in one place and states stay independent, but the context grows with every stage. Choose states-own when the per-stage logic is rich; context-own (or a table/enum) when the flow must be read and audited at a glance."},{"k":"sta-review:05","l":"P03","sec":"sta-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How do you persist the state in a database?","a":"<b>I ·</b> Store a stable <b>name</b> of the stage (<code>\"PAID\"</code>), not the object and not an enum ordinal. With JPA: an enum field with <code>@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)</code>, or a converter. On load, rebuild the state object from the name (<code>Status.valueOf</code>, or a small factory/map for class-based states). Any per-state data (refund amount, chassis) goes in normal columns. Add <code>@Version</code> so concurrent transitions are detected."},{"k":"sta-review:06","l":"P03","sec":"sta-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How do you test a State implementation?","a":"<b>I ·</b> Test each state on its own: put an order into that state, call every action, and check the resulting state name and output (or the exception). That gives a table \"state × action → expected\", which is exactly the state diagram; every missing arrow is a \"rejected\" test. Add one end-to-end test of the happy path. Because the context only delegates, it needs very few tests itself."},{"k":"sta-review:07","l":"P03","sec":"sta-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"When is a plain enum and a switch enough?","a":"<b>B ·</b> When there are few stages, one or two actions depend on them, and the list rarely changes, e.g. a <code>boolean</code>-like \"draft / published\". A switch over an <code>enum</code> (not a String) is type-safe, and a <code>switch</code> expression without <code>default</code> even forces you to handle a new constant. Move to State (or an enum with per-constant methods) when several methods switch on the same field."},{"k":"sta-review:08","l":"P03","sec":"sta-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Are state transitions thread-safe?","a":"<b>A ·</b> Not by themselves: \"read state, decide, write next state\" is a check-then-act race. If two threads call <code>pay()</code> and <code>cancel()</code> together, both can act on the old state. Guard the transition: <code>synchronized</code> methods on the context, an <code>AtomicReference&lt;OrderState&gt;</code> with <code>compareAndSet</code>, or, across servers, a database transaction with optimistic locking (<code>@Version</code>). Stateless shared state objects themselves are safe to share; states with fields belong to one order."},{"k":"fac-review:01","l":"P04","sec":"fac-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"What is the Factory Method pattern? (the 30-second answer)","a":"<b>B ·</b> Factory Method defines a method for creating an object in a base class, but lets subclasses decide which concrete class to instantiate. The base class has a business method that calls the abstract factory method and works with the result through an interface, so it never names a concrete class. Example: <code>Showroom.sellCar()</code> calls <code>createCar()</code>, which <code>CityShowroom</code> and <code>EvShowroom</code> override; in the JDK, <code>Collection.iterator()</code>."},{"k":"fac-review:02","l":"P04","sec":"fac-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Walk me through it in more depth.","a":"<b>I ·</b> Four roles: the <b>Product</b> interface (<code>Car</code>), <b>ConcreteProducts</b> implementing it, the <b>Creator</b> (<code>Showroom</code>) with the factory method and an operation that uses it, and <b>ConcreteCreators</b> that override the factory method. The client picks a creator. Benefits: Open / Closed (a new product is a new subclass), loose coupling to concrete classes, one place per product where <code>new</code> lives, and testing with a fake creator. Costs: a parallel hierarchy and the choice of creator still has to be made somewhere."},{"k":"fac-review:03","l":"P04","sec":"fac-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Simple Factory vs Factory Method vs Abstract Factory?","a":"<b>I ·</b> <b>Simple Factory</b> is one class or static method with a switch that returns a product; it is not a GoF pattern. <b>Factory Method</b> is an overridable method in a creator class: the subclass decides, so new products mean new subclasses. <b>Abstract Factory</b> is an interface with several factory methods that create a <i>family</i> of matching products (an India set vs an EU set); it is often implemented with factory methods. Rule of thumb: one product, switch on a parameter = simple factory; one product, subclass decides inside a routine = factory method; several related products that must match = abstract factory."},{"k":"fac-review:04","l":"P04","sec":"fac-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Static factory method vs the GoF Factory Method?","a":"<b>I ·</b> A static factory method (Effective Java item 1) is a named static method that returns an instance: <code>List.of</code>, <code>LocalDate.of</code>, <code>Integer.valueOf</code>. Pros: names, may return a cached or subtype instance, no new object needed, can hide implementation classes. Cons: cannot be overridden, so no polymorphic choice; classes with only static factories and private constructors cannot be subclassed; harder to discover in docs. The GoF Factory Method is an instance method that subclasses override. Same word, different mechanism."},{"k":"fac-review:05","l":"P04","sec":"fac-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How does it support Open / Closed and Dependency Inversion?","a":"<b>B ·</b> Open / Closed: the creator's routine is closed for modification, and a new product is added by a new subclass instead of another branch. Dependency Inversion: the high-level <code>Showroom</code> depends on the abstraction <code>Car</code>, and the concrete class is chosen by a subclass or injected from outside, so no high-level code names <code>PetrolCar</code>."},{"k":"fac-review:06","l":"P04","sec":"fac-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"What is a parameterized factory method?","a":"<b>I ·</b> A factory method that takes an argument (a type code, a string, an enum) and chooses the product: <code>Car createCar(String fuel)</code>. It is compact, but the switch inside has the same Open / Closed problem as a Simple Factory unless subclasses override it for new kinds, or the argument selects from a registry (<code>Map&lt;String, Supplier&lt;Car&gt;&gt;</code>). Good at the edge of the system, risky if it spreads."},{"k":"fac-review:07","l":"P04","sec":"fac-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How do you test code that uses a factory method?","a":"<b>I ·</b> Override the factory method in a test subclass (or an anonymous class) that returns a stub or mock product, so the business method runs without real engines, databases or network. With a static factory you cannot do this without a mocking library or a seam; that is one reason to prefer an overridable method or an injected <code>Supplier</code>."},{"k":"fac-review:08","l":"P04","sec":"fac-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"When would you NOT use Factory Method?","a":"<b>B ·</b> When there is one product and it does not vary, when a simple factory or a <code>Map</code> of suppliers is enough, or when you need matching families (Abstract Factory) or step-by-step construction (Builder). Adding a creator hierarchy for variation that never comes is over-engineering."},{"k":"fac-review:09","l":"P04","sec":"fac-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Why is calling a factory method from a constructor dangerous?","a":"<b>A ·</b> Java runs the base-class constructor before the subclass's fields are initialised, so an overridden factory method called there runs on a half-built subclass and can see null or default values. Call it lazily or from a normal method after construction, or pass the product in (injection)."},{"k":"bld-review:01","l":"P05","sec":"bld-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"What is the Builder pattern? (the 30-second answer)","a":"<b>B ·</b> Builder separates the construction of a complex object from its representation: you collect the options step by step in a builder, then <code>build()</code> returns the finished object. In Java it is usually a static nested class with fluent setters that return <code>this</code>, required values in its constructor, and a <code>build()</code> that validates and creates an immutable product. Example: <code>new Car.Builder(\"Nexon\",\"EV\").colour(\"Red\").sunroof(true).build()</code>; in the JDK, <code>HttpRequest.newBuilder()</code> and <code>StringBuilder</code>."},{"k":"bld-review:02","l":"P05","sec":"bld-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Walk me through it in more depth.","a":"<b>I ·</b> <code>Car</code> has final fields and a private constructor taking the builder, so the builder is the only way in. <code>Car.Builder</code> has the required values (final, set in its constructor), optional ones with defaults, one fluent method per option, and <code>build()</code> which checks every cross-field rule (sunroof not on Base trim, warranty 0 to 5) and then calls <code>new Car(this)</code>. Benefits: readable named options, no invalid objects, immutability and thread safety of the product, one place for rules, and new options without touching callers. Costs: extra boilerplate, a runtime (not compile-time) check for missing required values, one extra object per build."},{"k":"bld-review:03","l":"P05","sec":"bld-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Why a Builder instead of constructors or setters?","a":"<b>B ·</b> Long constructors are positional and unreadable (<code>true, false, true</code>) and need a new overload for each option (the telescoping constructor problem). JavaBeans setters are readable but leave the object mutable and possibly half-built, with no moment where the whole object is validated, and they are not safe to share between threads. A Builder keeps the named options of setters and the all-at-once, immutable result of a constructor."},{"k":"bld-review:04","l":"P05","sec":"bld-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How does it give you immutability and thread safety?","a":"<b>I ·</b> The product has only <code>final</code> fields, set in a private constructor, no setters, and defensive copies of any mutable parts. Once <code>build()</code> returns, nothing can change it, and final fields are safely published, so threads can share it without locks. The builder is mutable and is <b>not</b> thread-safe: use one per order."},{"k":"bld-review:05","l":"P05","sec":"bld-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Where should validation go: the setters, the constructor or build()?","a":"<b>I ·</b> In <code>build()</code> for rules across several fields (sunroof needs a non-Base trim): all values are known by then, and the order of the calls does not matter. Simple single-field checks may also go in the setter to fail early. Some teams also check in the product's constructor so that no path can skip the rule. Never rely on callers to check."},{"k":"bld-review:06","l":"P05","sec":"bld-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Builder vs Factory Method (and Abstract Factory)?","a":"<b>I ·</b> Factory Method decides <i>which class</i> to create, through an overridable method; one call, one product. Builder decides <i>how one complex object is assembled</i>, in steps, and the class is usually fixed. Abstract Factory creates a family of matching products. They combine: a factory method can return the result of a <code>build()</code>."},{"k":"bld-review:07","l":"P05","sec":"bld-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"What is the classic GoF Builder and the Director?","a":"<b>A ·</b> GoF has a <b>Builder</b> interface with step methods, <b>ConcreteBuilders</b> that implement them and keep the product being built, and a <b>Director</b> that calls the steps in a fixed order. The same Director can drive different builders to produce different representations (a <code>CarBuilder</code> and a <code>PriceSheetBuilder</code>). The fluent Builder in Effective Java drops the Director and the interface: the caller is the recipe. Use the GoF form only when you really need several representations."},{"k":"bld-review:08","l":"P05","sec":"bld-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How does Lombok's <code>@Builder</code> relate to this?","a":"<b>B ·</b> It is a library that generates the same fluent builder class at compile time from annotations, so you do not write the boilerplate. It does not add your cross-field rules by itself (write them in a custom <code>build</code> or the constructor), and it adds a dependency. Know how to write the builder by hand: interviewers ask."},{"k":"bld-review:09","l":"P05","sec":"bld-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How do you handle required fields, and what is a step builder?","a":"<b>A ·</b> Put required values in the builder's constructor, or check them in <code>build()</code>. A step builder returns a different interface after each required step, so the compiler rejects an incomplete chain: safer, but more code. Records do not have optional fields, so combine a record with a builder if you need both immutability and options."},{"k":"bld-review:10","l":"P05","sec":"bld-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How would you copy an object with one field changed?","a":"<b>I ·</b> Add <code>toBuilder()</code> (copy-with): it creates a builder pre-filled from the product; change one option and call <code>build()</code> again, so the rules are re-checked. For a record, a <code>withColour(...)</code> method that calls the canonical constructor does the same."},{"k":"sgl-review:01","l":"P06","sec":"sgl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"What is the Singleton pattern? (the 30-second answer)","a":"<b>B ·</b> Singleton ensures a class has <b>exactly one instance</b> and gives a global access point to it. In Java: a <code>private</code> constructor, a <code>private static</code> field holding the instance, and a <code>public static getInstance()</code>. Example: the dealership's <code>VehicleRegistry</code>, shared by the sales desk, service desk and reports; in the JDK, <code>Runtime.getRuntime()</code>."},{"k":"sgl-review:02","l":"P06","sec":"sgl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Walk me through the thread-safe options.","a":"<b>I ·</b> 1) <b>Eager</b> <code>static final</code> field: simplest, thread-safe because class loading is. 2) Lazy with <b><code>synchronized</code> getInstance()</b>: correct, but every call takes the lock. 3) <b>Double-checked locking</b> with a <code>volatile</code> field: lock only while the field is null. 4) <b>Holder idiom</b>: a private static nested class holds the instance, loaded on first use, lazy and lock-free. 5) <b>Enum</b>: one constant, also safe against reflection and serialization. In practice: eager or holder, or let Spring manage it."},{"k":"sgl-review:03","l":"P06","sec":"sgl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Why must the field be <code>volatile</code> in double-checked locking?","a":"<b>A ·</b> <code>instance = new X()</code> is allocate, run the constructor, assign the reference, and the compiler or CPU may reorder the last two. A thread passing the unlocked first check could then see a non-null reference to an object whose constructor has not finished. <code>volatile</code> forbids that reordering (a happens-before edge between the write and later reads) and makes the value visible to all threads. Without it the code is broken even if it usually works."},{"k":"sgl-review:04","l":"P06","sec":"sgl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"What are the pros and cons of an enum singleton?","a":"<b>I ·</b> <b>Pros:</b> shortest code, thread-safe, serialization-safe (the JVM guarantees one constant), reflection cannot create another (\"Cannot reflectively create enum objects\"). <b>Cons:</b> cannot extend a class (interfaces are fine), eager-ish, no constructor arguments from outside, and awkward to mock; the style can surprise teammates. Joshua Bloch recommends it in Effective Java."},{"k":"sgl-review:05","l":"P06","sec":"sgl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How can a Singleton be broken, and how do you stop it?","a":"<b>A ·</b> <b>Reflection:</b> <code>setAccessible(true)</code> on the private constructor; guard in the constructor, or use an enum. <b>Serialization:</b> deserializing creates a new object; add <code>readResolve()</code> returning the instance, or use an enum. <b>Cloning:</b> only if the class is <code>Cloneable</code>; do not implement it, or throw from <code>clone()</code>. <b>Several class loaders:</b> each can load its own copy. <b>Several threads:</b> an unsafe lazy version."},{"k":"sgl-review:06","l":"P06","sec":"sgl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Singleton vs a static utility class?","a":"<b>B ·</b> A static class has no object: only static methods, no interface, no polymorphism, and its state is hard to replace. A Singleton is a real object, so it can implement an interface, be passed around and (with care) be mocked, and it can be created lazily. Use a static class for pure, stateless helpers (like <code>Math</code>); use a Singleton (or better an injected bean) when there is one shared object with state."},{"k":"sgl-review:07","l":"P06","sec":"sgl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Singleton vs a Spring singleton-scoped bean?","a":"<b>I ·</b> The GoF Singleton is enforced by the class: one per class loader (about one per JVM), reached by a static method. A Spring singleton bean is one instance <b>per <code>ApplicationContext</code></b>, created and injected by the container; the class itself is an ordinary class that could be instantiated twice. Two contexts mean two beans. Spring's is easier to test and has no global name."},{"k":"sgl-review:08","l":"P06","sec":"sgl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Why is Singleton called an anti-pattern, and what are the testing problems?","a":"<b>I ·</b> It is global mutable state with a hidden dependency: a method calls <code>getInstance()</code> inside, so its signature does not show it, tests cannot replace the object, and state leaks from one test to the next. It also couples callers to a concrete class and complicates concurrency."},{"k":"sgl-review:09","l":"P06","sec":"sgl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"How do you fix the testing problem?","a":"<b>I ·</b> Depend on an <b>interface</b> (<code>StockLookup</code>) and pass the object in through the constructor (dependency injection). Production code wires the one real instance once; tests pass a fake. Keep \"one\" as a decision of the application (one bean), not of the class."},{"k":"sgl-review:10","l":"P06","sec":"sgl-review","st":"Interview and review","q":"Eager or lazy: how do you choose?","a":"<b>B ·</b> Eager is simplest and thread-safe; choose it when creation is cheap or the object is always needed. Choose lazy (holder idiom) when creation is expensive or may never be needed, or when it depends on something not ready at class load. A failing eager constructor breaks class loading, which gives an <code>ExceptionInInitializerError</code>."}]};
